Hinds County (Jackson): about 9,000 regular ballots still expected, where Colom leads the counted vote by 61 points. If Hinds turns out to be nearly finished, Colom's chance falls to about 82%.", "Rankin County: about 6,000 still expected, where Hyde-Smith leads by 26 points. It is the biggest offset to Hinds.", "Affidavit (provisional) ballots: 0 of 82 counties have reported official counts so far; the rest are estimated.", "Voters who cast an affidavit ballot for lack of photo ID have five business days (to about Nov. 10) to show ID at the circuit clerk's office. Mail ballots postmarked by Nov. 3 count if they arrive by then too.", "Mississippi has no automatic recount and no runoff in this race. A recount needs a court order."], "changed": {"since": "8 AM", "head": "Colom's chance held at 97%; most likely final margin Colom +10,200.", "items": ["About 2,014 new votes were counted. The lead in counted votes went from Colom +1,138 to Colom +1,724. Most came from Lauderdale (1,925 votes, Colom +511); Claiborne (82 votes, Colom +68); Jefferson (7 votes, Colom +7).", "AP changed its estimate of ballots left to count in 10 counties (47,050 to 47,050 statewide). Biggest effects: Claiborne, 400 fewer (Colom +76 pts there); Washington, 450 more (Colom +56 pts there); Lowndes, 400 fewer (Colom +16 pts there). Together these shift the expected outcome about 90 votes toward Hyde-Smith."]}, "summary": "Colom is favored, but the race is not over. Colom leads by 1,724 votes with about 47,000 ballots left to count. The biggest batch is in Hinds County (about 9,000), where Colom is winning about 4 in 5 votes.", "page_version": "ca50ffb0"}